Task 1: Automating OSD Addition
Step 1.1: Create the OSD Addition Playbook
tee ~/ceph-ansible-playbooks/add-osd.yml << 'EOF'
---
- name: Add a New OSD to the Ceph Cluster
  hosts: osds
  become: yes
  gather_facts: yes
  vars:
    osd_disk: "/dev/sdb"

  tasks:
    - name: Install required packages
      apt:
        name:
          - ceph-osd
          - ceph-common
          - gdisk
          - parted
        state: present
        update_cache: yes

    - name: Check whether the target disk already has an OSD prepared
      shell: |
        ceph-volume lvm list {{ osd_disk }}
      register: osd_check
      changed_when: false
      failed_when: false

    - name: Prepare and activate the disk as a new OSD
      shell: |
        ceph-volume lvm create --data {{ osd_disk }}
      register: osd_create_result
      when: "'Devices' not in osd_check.stdout"

    - name: Wait for the new OSD to be reported up
      shell: |
        ceph osd tree
      register: osd_status
      until: "'up' in osd_status.stdout"
      retries: 15
      delay: 10
      delegate_to: "{{ groups['mons'][0] }}"
      when: osd_create_result is changed

    - name: Display cluster OSD tree
      debug:
        msg: "{{ osd_status.stdout_lines }}"
      when: osd_status is defined
EOF
Step 1.2: Run the Playbook Against a Single Node
Run this against one OSD host at a time so the disk path applies correctly per host. In this lab environment, /dev/sdb is a pre-attached blank disk on each Ceph node reserved for training.

ansible-playbook add-osd.yml --check --diff --limit ceph-node2

ansible-playbook add-osd.yml --limit ceph-node2 -e "osd_disk=/dev/sdb"

ansible mons[0] -m shell -a "ceph osd tree" -i hosts.yml
Deliverable: The --check run completes with no errors, the real run reports changed for the ceph-volume lvm create task, and ceph osd tree shows a new osd.N line with status up and in under the host you targeted.

Task 2: Automating Pool Creation and CRUSH Map Management
Ceph pools and CRUSH rules are tightly linked: every pool is bound to exactly one CRUSH rule, and that rule determines where (which OSDs, hosts, or racks) the pool's data is placed and how many failure domains it spans. A CRUSH rule is cluster-wide configuration â it describes a placement strategy (for example, "replicate across hosts using only SSD-class OSDs") â while a pool is a named container that references one rule via its crush_rule property. Multiple pools can share the same rule, but a single pool can only use one rule at a time.

Use a pool-specific approach (just creating/configuring the pool) when default placement across all hosts is acceptable. Use a CRUSH-level change (new rule, device class, or bucket) when you need to steer placement, for example isolating a pool to SSD-backed OSDs only. The relationship looks like this:

CRUSH Map                          Pools
-----------                        -----------------
rule "replicated_rule" (default) <--- pool "rbd"
rule "ssd_rule" (host, class=ssd) <--- pool "fast-data"
rule "hdd_rule"  (host, class=hdd) <--- pool "bulk-archive"
In this task you will first create a CRUSH rule, then create a pool that explicitly uses it, demonstrating the full chain from CRUSH rule to pool assignment.

Step 2.1: Create the CRUSH Map Management Playbook
tee ~/ceph-ansible-playbooks/manage-crush-map.yml << 'EOF'
---
- name: Manage Ceph CRUSH Map Rules
  hosts: mons[0]
  become: yes
  gather_facts: yes
  vars:
    crush_backup_dir: /tmp/crush-backups
    rule_name: "ssd_rule"
    failure_domain: "host"

  tasks:
    - name: Create backup directory
      file:
        path: "{{ crush_backup_dir }}"
        state: directory
        mode: '0755'

    - name: Backup current CRUSH map (binary and JSON)
      shell: |
        ceph osd getcrushmap -o {{ crush_backup_dir }}/crushmap-{{ ansible_date_time.epoch }}.bin
        ceph osd crush dump > {{ crush_backup_dir }}/crushmap-{{ ansible_date_time.epoch }}.json

    - name: List existing CRUSH rules
      shell: ceph osd crush rule ls
      register: existing_rules
      changed_when: false

    - name: Create a replicated CRUSH rule scoped to a device class
      shell: |
        ceph osd crush rule create-replicated {{ rule_name }} default {{ failure_domain }} {{ device_class | default('ssd') }}
      when: rule_name not in existing_rules.stdout

    - name: Verify the new rule exists
      shell: ceph osd crush rule ls
      register: updated_rules
      changed_when: false

    - name: Display current CRUSH rules
      debug:
        msg: "{{ updated_rules.stdout_lines }}"

    - name: Show the rule's full definition
      shell: ceph osd crush rule dump {{ rule_name }}
      register: rule_dump
      changed_when: false

    - name: Display rule definition
      debug:
        msg: "{{ rule_dump.stdout }}"
EOF
Step 2.2: Create the Pool Management Playbook
tee ~/ceph-ansible-playbooks/manage-pools.yml << 'EOF'
---
- name: Manage Ceph Pools
  hosts: mons[0]
  become: yes
  gather_facts: no
  vars:
    pool_name: "fast-data"
    pg_num: 32
    pgp_num: 32
    pool_size: 3
    pool_min_size: 2
    crush_rule: "ssd_rule"
    pool_application: "rbd"

  tasks:
    - name: Check current cluster health
      shell: ceph -s --format json
      register: cluster_status
      changed_when: false

    - name: Parse cluster health status
      set_fact:
        cluster_health: "{{ (cluster_status.stdout | from_json).health.status }}"

    - name: Fail if cluster is not healthy
      fail:
        msg: "Cluster health is {{ cluster_health }}. Resolve before creating pools."
      when:
        - cluster_health != "HEALTH_OK"
        - force_create | default(false) == false

    - name: Check if pool already exists
      shell: ceph osd pool ls | grep -x "{{ pool_name }}"
      register: pool_exists
      failed_when: false
      changed_when: false

    - name: Create the pool
      shell: |
        ceph osd pool create {{ pool_name }} {{ pg_num }} {{ pgp_num }} replicated
      when: pool_exists.rc != 0

    - name: Assign the pool to a specific CRUSH rule
      shell: |
        ceph osd pool set {{ pool_name }} crush_rule {{ crush_rule }}
      register: rule_assignment
      failed_when:
        - rule_assignment.rc != 0
        - "'does not exist' in rule_assignment.stderr"

    - name: Set pool replication size
      shell: ceph osd pool set {{ pool_name }} size {{ pool_size }}

    - name: Set pool min_size
      shell: ceph osd pool set {{ pool_name }} min_size {{ pool_min_size }}

    - name: Enable application on the pool
      shell: ceph osd pool application enable {{ pool_name }} {{ pool_application }}
      register: app_enable
      failed_when:
        - app_enable.rc != 0
        - "'already enabled' not in app_enable.stderr"

    - name: Retrieve final pool configuration
      shell: ceph osd pool get {{ pool_name }} crush_rule
      register: final_rule
      changed_when: false

    - name: Display assigned CRUSH rule for the pool
      debug:
        msg: "Pool {{ pool_name }} is using CRUSH rule: {{ final_rule.stdout }}"
EOF
Step 2.3: Combine Both Playbooks into a Single Deployment
Create an orchestrating playbook that runs the CRUSH rule setup first, then creates the pool bound to that rule, proving the dependency order matters.

tee ~/ceph-ansible-playbooks/deploy-storage-solution.yml << 'EOF'
---
- name: Deploy Complete Storage Solution (CRUSH Rule + Pool)
  hosts: mons[0]
  become: yes
  gather_facts: yes
  vars:
    rule_name: "ssd_rule"
    device_class: "ssd"
    failure_domain: "host"
    pool_name: "fast-data"
    pg_num: 32
    pgp_num: 32
    pool_size: 3
    pool_min_size: 2
    pool_application: "rbd"

  tasks:
    - name: Include CRUSH map management tasks
      import_playbook: manage-crush-map.yml
      vars:
        rule_name: "{{ rule_name }}"
        device_class: "{{ device_class }}"
        failure_domain: "{{ failure_domain }}"

    - name: Include pool management tasks
      import_playbook: manage-pools.yml
      vars:
        pool_name: "{{ pool_name }}"
        pg_num: "{{ pg_num }}"
        pgp_num: "{{ pgp_num }}"
        pool_size: "{{ pool_size }}"
        pool_min_size: "{{ pool_min_size }}"
        crush_rule: "{{ rule_name }}"
        pool_application: "{{ pool_application }}"
EOF
Note: import_playbook can only be used at the top level of a playbook file, not nested inside a tasks: list. Replace the file above with the corrected top-level structure below, which is the version you will actually execute:

tee ~/ceph-ansible-playbooks/deploy-storage-solution.yml << 'EOF'
---
- import_playbook: manage-crush-map.yml
  vars:
    rule_name: "ssd_rule"
    device_class: "ssd"
    failure_domain: "host"

- import_playbook: manage-pools.yml
  vars:
    pool_name: "fast-data"
    pg_num: 32
    pgp_num: 32
    pool_size: 3
    pool_min_size: 2
    crush_rule: "ssd_rule"
    pool_application: "rbd"
EOF
