Troubleshooting
Symptom: ceph-volume lvm create fails with "Device is not available" or similar. Diagnostic hint: Run ceph-volume lvm list and lsblk on the target host to confirm the disk path is correct and has no existing partition table or LVM signature from a prior test.

Symptom: ceph osd pool set fast-data crush_rule ssd_rule fails with an error about the rule not existing or having an incompatible bucket type. Diagnostic hint: Run ceph osd crush rule ls and ceph osd tree to confirm the rule was actually created and that OSDs in your cluster have the expected device class assigned (ceph osd crush class ls).

Conclusion
In this lab, students automated core Ceph cluster operations using Ansible, moving from manual command-line administration to repeatable, version-controlled playbooks. Students built and executed a playbook to add a new OSD safely, then explored the relationship between CRUSH rules and pools by creating a device-class-scoped CRUSH rule and binding a new pool to it explicitly, verifying the linkage with ceph osd pool get and ceph osd crush rule dump. Finally, students combined both playbooks into a single orchestrated deployment, demonstrating how independent Ansible playbooks can be composed into larger, reusable automation workflows.

Key skills gained include writing idempotent Ansible tasks against a live Ceph cluster, safely gating destructive or state-changing operations behind health checks, and understanding how CRUSH rules and pools interact to control data placement. Students who want to extend this work further should explore automating erasure-coded pool creation, multi-rule CRUSH hierarchies spanning racks or datacenters, and integrating these playbooks with cephadm or the official ceph-ansible roles for full lifecycle management in production-like environments.
